# Ruby on Rails patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Rails application design and its common anti-patterns. Applies only to
`*.rb` files.

## MVC boundaries

- A controller action orchestrates: it reads params, calls into a
  model/service, and renders/redirects — it does not contain business
  logic (validation rules, multi-step workflows, external API calls)
  inline in the action body.
- A view renders presentation only; move any non-trivial conditional or
  data-shaping logic in a view into a helper or a presenter/decorator
  object instead of embedded Ruby blocks doing real work.
- `ApplicationRecord` models hold persistence concerns (validations,
  associations, scopes) and behavior that genuinely belongs to the
  record itself; a model accumulating callbacks that orchestrate
  unrelated side effects (send an email, call a third-party API, update
  a different model) is a "fat model" — extract that orchestration into
  a service object or an `ActiveJob`.

## Concerns

- A `Concern` (`app/models/concerns`, `app/controllers/concerns`) should
  bundle genuinely reusable, cohesive behavior shared by more than one
  class — not become a dumping ground for one model's unrelated methods
  split out just to shrink the file. If nothing else in the app is ever
  going to `include` it, it is not a concern, it is the class's own
  method.
- Name a concern for the *capability* it adds (`Archivable`,
  `Sluggable`), not for the model it happens to have been extracted
  from.

## Service objects and fat controllers/models

- When an action's logic spans multiple models, calls an external
  service, or has enough branching to need its own tests independent of
  the controller, extract a plain Ruby service object (a
  `SomethingVerb` class with one public entry point, e.g. `.call`) that
  the controller/model delegates to — do not grow the controller action
  or a model callback to hold it, and match the app's own existing
  service-object convention (directory, base class, `.call` vs `#call`)
  if one already exists rather than inventing a second style.
- A service object should be easy to unit test without spinning up a
  full request — keep framework concerns (params, session, rendering)
  out of it; pass it plain values or model instances instead.

## ActiveRecord query patterns

- Eager-load an association before iterating over it in a loop
  (`Book.includes(:author)`), not after the N+1 has already happened —
  prefer `includes` (Rails picks `preload` or `eager_load` for you) over
  hand-choosing one of the lower-level methods unless you have a
  specific reason to force one.
- Push filtering/aggregation into the database (`where`, `group`,
  `count`, a named `scope`) rather than loading full ActiveRecord
  objects into Ruby and filtering/reducing them in memory.
- A named `scope` (`scope :published, -> { where(published: true) }`)
  for a query fragment reused in more than one place, instead of
  copy-pasting the same `where` clause across controllers/models.

## Background jobs

- An `ActiveJob` should be idempotent — most production queue adapters
  (Sidekiq, SQS, Resque) are at-least-once, so a retried or duplicated job run must not
  double-charge, double-send, or otherwise corrupt state; guard with a
  uniqueness check, an idempotency key, or an upsert instead of assuming
  `perform` runs exactly once.
- Pass simple identifiers (an id) into a job's `perform`, not a whole
  ActiveRecord object — the record can change or be deleted between
  enqueue and execution (GlobalID serializes it, but the job should
  re-fetch and handle a missing record rather than trust a stale copy).

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "fat controller" action with inline business logic, or a "fat
  model" whose callbacks orchestrate unrelated side effects — extract a
  service object per above.
- A concern used purely to split up one class's file size rather than to
  share real behavior.
- Raw SQL string interpolation instead of ActiveRecord's parameterized
  query interface or `sanitize_sql` (see `rules/security.mdc`).
- A `default_scope` that silently changes every query against a model —
  usually surprising at a call site far from the model definition;
  prefer an explicit named scope callers opt into.
